On every platform but macOS, a plain Page Up/Down in a text control
moves the cursor by a page in Ladybird, as it does in Blink, whose
editing_behavior.cc maps the plain VKEY_NEXT and VKEY_PRIOR keys
to MovePageDown and MovePageUp everywhere except on macOS, where
Option+Page Down moves instead. Gecko’s ShortcutKeyDefinitions.cpp
maps VK_PAGE_DOWN to cmd_movePageDown on Windows and Linux and to
cmd_scrollPageDown on macOS, and WebKitGTK’s KeyBindingTranslator maps
GDK_KEY_Page_Down to MovePageDown. Run headless on macOS (Chrome 158,
Firefox 151, WebKit 26.5), all three scrolled a textarea on a plain Page
Down without moving its caret, and moved the caret on Option+Page Down.

After the move, Ladybird scrolls the control by the same page, so
the cursor keeps its place in the view. Gecko does too: Run headless,
Firefox kept the caret on the view’s first row, and on its last
row. Blink reveals the caret with ScrollAlignment::TopAlways after
MoveCommands::ModifySelectionWithPageGranularity(), so from the view’s
first row, it ends the same way. On macOS, Chrome and WebKit center the
caret’s line after Option+Page Down instead; Ladybird doesn’t.

In a single-line input, a plain Page Up/Down still scrolls the page, as
in both engines: Gecko’s sInputHandlers in ShortcutKeyDefinitions.cpp
has no Page Down binding, so the key reaches the browser’s page
scrolling, and Blink’s MoveCommands::VerticalScrollDistance() returns 0
for a focused input, which is neither editable itself nor scrollable, so
MovePageDown isn’t handled.
